\title{M\"obius Arithmetic: Orientation Twists of the Integers,\\ Their Spectra, and Their Zeta Functions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study number systems obtained by imposing an orientation ($\mathbb{Z}/2$) symmetry on the integers, in two essentially different ways, and determine completely what arithmetic invariants each construction produces.

The first construction is \emph{set-theoretic}: the \emph{M\"obius integers} $\Zt$ are the oriented integers $\mathbb{Z}\times\{\pm1\}$ modulo the M\"obius identification $(n,+1)\sim(-n,-1)$, with arithmetic performed through the identification. We prove that the quotient map $\mathbb{Z}\times\{\pm1\}\to\Zt$ is a genuine free double cover --- the deck involution $(n,\varepsilon)\mapsto(-n,-\varepsilon)$ is fixed-point free and every fibre has exactly two points --- yet the resulting ring is isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}$. Consequently: the class number is one; prime \emph{elements} double-cover the rational primes as a $\mathbb{Z}/2$-torsor, while the prime \emph{spectrum} does not double at all; the integer $6$ has exactly four ordered factorizations into M\"obius primes, yet unique factorization holds up to orientation with the multiset of radii a complete invariant; the Ore condition holds; and the Dirichlet series is $\zt(s)=2\zeta(s)$, so the M\"obius Riemann hypothesis is \emph{equivalent} to the classical one. We prove a general theorem subsuming the latter: any norm whose nonzero fibres all have cardinality $k$ has Dirichlet series $k\zeta(s)$, with Riemann hypothesis equivalent to the classical one for every $k\ge1$.

We then isolate the structural reason. Multiplication lifts to the cover \emph{separably}, while addition admits no such lift, in a sharp sense: even allowing the orientation of a sum to be an arbitrary function of both complete oriented inputs, the magnitude of the sum is not a function of the two magnitudes. We give a necessary criterion for separable liftability, show it rules out addition, subtraction and many other operations, and exhibit an operation showing the criterion is not sufficient. We also classify the possible twists: the only additive symmetry of $\mathbb{Z}$ of finite order is $\pm\mathrm{id}$, negation occurring only in even order.

The second construction is \emph{multiplicative}: the \emph{oriented double} $\Oo=\mathbb{Z}[\tau]/(\tau^2-1)$, realised as the index-two subring $\{(u,v)\in\mathbb{Z}^2 : u\equiv v \bmod 2\}$ of $\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}$. Here every prediction that failed for $\Zt$ succeeds: $\Oo$ is not a domain; its unit group is the Klein four-group; it has exactly two ring maps to $\mathbb{Z}$, exchanged by the deck involution; and $\Spec\Oo\to\Spec\mathbb{Z}$ is a double cover branched exactly at $2$, with conductor equal to the branch locus. Its spectral zeta function is $\zeta_{\Oo}(s)=\zeta(s)^2(1-2^{-s})$, with Dirichlet coefficients $d(n)-d(n/2)$ counting ideals of index $n$ at prime index, and value $\pi^4/48$ at $s=2$. The ramified factor produces a zero at $s_0=2\pi i/\log 2$ which is not a zero of $\zeta$; so the oriented Riemann hypothesis is false, while inside the critical strip it remains equivalent to the classical one.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

The M\"obius band is the quotient of the annulus $S^1\times[-1,1]$ by the free involution $(\theta,t)\mapsto(\theta+\pi,-t)$: the simplest nontrivial free $\mathbb{Z}/2$-cover, and the prototype of orientation reversal.

Arithmetic is also organised by quotients and covers: $\mathbb{Z}/n$, ideal class groups, extensions of number fields with their splitting and ramification, and the Euler products that encode all of this analytically. It is therefore natural to ask whether the M\"obius twist can be transplanted into number theory: take the integers, remember an orientation, glue with a half-turn, and see what arithmetic emerges.

This paper carries out that programme, tests the conjectures it suggests, and explains the pattern of successes and failures by locating precisely where a $\mathbb{Z}/2$ symmetry must be stored in order to have arithmetic consequences.

\subsection{Notation}

$\zeta$ is the Riemann zeta function, $d=\sigma_0$ the divisor-counting function, $\mathbb{F}_p=\mathbb{Z}/p$, and $\Spec R$ the prime spectrum of a commutative ring $R$. ``Radius'' and ``norm'' are used interchangeably for the absolute value of the signed value of a M\"obius integer.

\section{The M\"obius integers as a free double cover}

\begin{definition}[Oriented integers]
An \emph{oriented integer} is a pair $a=(n,\varepsilon)$ with $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ (the \emph{magnitude}) and $\varepsilon\in\{\pm1\}$ (the \emph{orientation}). Write $\Or=\mathbb{Z}\times\{\pm1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Signed value]
The \emph{signed value} of $a=(n,\varepsilon)$ is $v(a)=\varepsilon n\in\mathbb{Z}$; thus $v(n,+1)=n$ and $v(n,-1)=-n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[M\"obius identification]
Declare $a\sim b$ iff $v(a)=v(b)$. This is an equivalence relation whose defining instance is
\[(n,+1)\;\sim\;(-n,-1).\]
The set of classes is the set of \emph{M\"obius integers} $\Zt=\Or/\!\sim$. Write $[a]$ for the class of $a$ and $V:\Zt\to\mathbb{Z}$, $V([a])=v(a)$, for the induced signed-value map. For $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ put $n^{+}=[(n,+1)]$ and $n^{-}=[(n,-1)]$, so $V(n^{+})=n$ and $V(n^{-})=-n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Vbij}
$V$ is a bijection $\Zt\to\mathbb{Z}$; equivalently $[a]=[b]\iff v(a)=v(b)$, and every integer is a signed value.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity is the definition of the relation after passing to representatives; surjectivity holds since $V(n^{+})=n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The deck involution}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{deck transformation} is $\tau:\Or\to\Or$, $\tau(n,\varepsilon)=(-n,-\varepsilon)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Free double cover]\label{thm:cover}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\tau\circ\tau=\mathrm{id}$.
\item $\tau(a)\ne a$ for every $a$: the involution is fixed-point free.
\item $[a]=[b]$ if and only if $b=a$ or $b=\tau(a)$.
\item Every fibre of $\Or\to\Zt$ has exactly two elements.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) is immediate. (2): $(-n,-\varepsilon)=(n,\varepsilon)$ forces $\varepsilon=-\varepsilon$. (3): $v(\tau(a))=(-\varepsilon)(-n)=v(a)$ gives one direction; conversely if $v(n,\varepsilon)=v(m,\delta)$ then either $\delta=\varepsilon$, forcing $m=n$, or $\delta=-\varepsilon$, forcing $m=-n$. (4) follows: the fibre through $a$ is $\{a,\tau(a)\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Orbit space]
Let $\mathbb{Z}/2$ act on $\Or$ with the nontrivial element acting as $\tau$. The action is free, and the orbit space is canonically $\Zt$.
\end{corollary}

This is the algebraic transcription of ``the M\"obius band is the annulus modulo a free involution'': free, unramified, with orbit space the band.

\subsection{Twisted arithmetic}

\begin{definition}\label{def:arith}
Define, for $x,y\in\Zt$,
\[x+y:=(V(x)+V(y))^{+},\qquad xy:=(V(x)V(y))^{+},\qquad -x:=(-V(x))^{+},\]
with $0=0^{+}$ and $1=1^{+}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:ring}
These operations are well defined, satisfy $V(x+y)=V(x)+V(y)$, $V(xy)=V(x)V(y)$, $V(0)=0$, $V(1)=1$, and make $\Zt$ a commutative ring.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Well-definedness is automatic since the operations are defined via the well-defined map $V$. Each ring axiom is pulled back along the injective map $V$ from the corresponding axiom of $\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Orientation reversal is negation]
$[(n,-\varepsilon)]=-[(n,\varepsilon)]$ for all $n,\varepsilon$; in particular $n^{-}=-n^{+}$.
\end{lemma}

\section{Structure: the twist collapses}

\begin{theorem}[Structure Theorem]\label{thm:structure}
The signed-value map is a ring isomorphism $V:\Zt\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\mathbb{Z}$, with inverse $n\mapsto n^{+}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemma \ref{lem:Vbij} and Proposition \ref{prop:ring}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\Zt$ is an integral domain and a principal ideal domain.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The unit group is the orientation group]
$x\in\Zt$ is a unit iff $x=\pm1$, and $-1\ne1$. Hence $\Zt^{\times}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2$.
\end{proposition}

So the twist survives \emph{only} as the unit group --- the same $\mathbb{Z}/2$ that is $\pi_1$ of the M\"obius band and that acted freely upstairs.

\subsection{Polar decomposition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{norm} (radius) of $x\in\Zt$ is $N(x)=|V(x)|\in\mathbb{N}$.
\end{definition}

Then $N(xy)=N(x)N(y)$, $N(-x)=N(x)$, and $N(x)=0\iff x=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Polar decomposition]\label{thm:polar}
The map $\Zt^{\times}\times\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\to\Zt\setminus\{0\}$, $(u,n)\mapsto u\cdot n^{+}$, is a multiplicative monoid isomorphism. Hence
\[\Zt\setminus\{0\}\;\cong\;\mathbb{Z}/2\times\mathbb{Z}_{>0}.\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplicativity follows from $(um^{+})(vn^{+})=(uv)(mn)^{+}$. Injectivity: applying $N$ gives $m=n$, and cancelling the nonzero $n^{+}$ in a domain gives $u=v$. Surjectivity: for $x\ne0$ put $n=N(x)$; then $x=1\cdot n^{+}$ or $x=(-1)\cdot n^{+}$ according to the sign of $V(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Multiplicative triviality]\label{cor:split}
The orientation character $\Zt\setminus\{0\}\to\mathbb{Z}/2$ is a surjective monoid homomorphism with section $n\mapsto n^{+}$: the M\"obius cover is multiplicatively a \emph{trivial} $\mathbb{Z}/2$-bundle over the monoid of radii.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Torsor structure of norm fibres]\label{thm:torsor}
If $x\ne0$ and $N(x)=N(y)$ then there is a \emph{unique} unit $u$ with $y=ux$. Each nonzero fibre of $N$ is a torsor under $\Zt^{\times}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Lattice count]
For $N\in\mathbb{N}$, exactly $2N+1$ M\"obius integers have norm $\le N$: two per positive radius, plus the centre.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Doubled divisor function]
For $x\ne0$, the set of divisors of $x$ in $\Zt$ has exactly $2\,d(N(x))$ elements.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divisibility transports along $V$; the divisors of a nonzero integer $m$ are the $\pm k$ with $k\mid|m|$, giving $2d(|m|)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Factorization theory, and the fate of the conjectures}

\begin{theorem}[Primality is orientation-blind]
$x\in\Zt$ is prime iff $N(x)$ is a rational prime. In particular $p^{+}$ and $p^{-}$ are prime for every rational prime $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Associates are orientation flips]\label{thm:assoc}
$x$ and $y$ are associates iff $y=x$ or $y=-x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fibres of the norm]\label{thm:fibres}
$\{x : N(x)=n\}=\{n^{+},n^{-}\}$, of size $2$ for $n\ne0$ and $\{0\}$ for $n=0$. Hence for a rational prime $p$,
\[\bigl|\{x\in\Zt : x\text{ prime},\ N(x)=p\}\bigr|=2 .\]
\end{theorem}

Prime \emph{elements} therefore double-cover the rational primes, and by Theorem \ref{thm:torsor} the two primes over $p$ form a $\mathbb{Z}/2$-torsor.

\begin{theorem}[No spectral doubling]\label{thm:nospec}
$(n^{+})=(n^{-})$ as ideals for every $n$; so $p^{+}$ and $p^{-}$, though distinct elements, define the same point of $\Spec\Zt$. Moreover $\Spec\Zt\cong\Spec\mathbb{Z}$ as ordered sets.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$n^{-}=-n^{+}$ and $-1$ is a unit; the second claim is functoriality of $\Spec$ applied to Theorem \ref{thm:structure}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Class number one]
$\Zt$ is a principal ideal domain, so its ideal class group is trivial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unique factorization up to orientation]\label{thm:uf}
If $f,g$ are finite multisets of primes of $\Zt$ with equal products, there is a bijection between $f$ and $g$ matching each $x$ with a $y$ satisfying $y=x$ or $y=-x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Zt$ is a unique factorization domain, so the multisets match up to associates; by Theorem \ref{thm:assoc} associates are exactly orientation flips.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Radii are a complete invariant]\label{cor:radii}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem \ref{thm:uf}, the multisets of norms of $f$ and $g$ coincide.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Factoring $6$]\label{thm:six}
The set of ordered pairs of M\"obius primes with product $6$ is exactly
\[\{(2^{+},3^{+}),\ (3^{+},2^{+}),\ (2^{-},3^{-}),\ (3^{-},2^{-})\},\]
of size four. In particular $6=2^{+}3^{+}=2^{-}3^{-}$ with $(2^{+},3^{+})\ne(2^{-},3^{-})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport to $\mathbb{Z}$: if $ab=6$ with $a,b$ prime then $|a||b|=6$ with both $\ge2$, forcing $\{|a|,|b|\}=\{2,3\}$, and the common sign is determined by positivity of the product.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$2^{+}$ and $2^{-}$ are associates, as are $3^{+}$ and $3^{-}$; unique factorization is not violated.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Ore condition]
For nonzero $a,b\in\Zt$ there exist nonzero $x,y$ with $ax=by\ne0$ (e.g.\ $x=b$, $y=a$). Hence $\Zt$ satisfies both Ore conditions.
\end{theorem}

\section{The M\"obius zeta function}

\begin{definition}
For $s\in\mathbb{C}$ set $\zt(s):=2\zeta(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Dirichlet series of $\Zt$]\label{thm:zt}
For $\Re s>1$,
\[\sum_{\substack{x\in\Zt\\ x\ne0}}\frac{1}{N(x)^{s}}\;=\;2\zeta(s)\;=\;\zt(s).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reindex along $V$ to $\sum_{n\ne0}|n|^{-s}$ and split into the positive and negative halves, each $\zeta(s)$ by absolute convergence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Euler product]
For $\Re s>1$, $\zt(s)=2\prod_p(1-p^{-s})^{-1}$: each rational prime contributes one factor, because its two oriented primes are associate and generate the same ideal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$\zt(s)=0\iff\zeta(s)=0$; in particular $\zt$ vanishes at $s=-2,-4,\dots$
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Zeros off the critical line, with a caveat]
There exist $s$ with $\zt(s)=0$ and $\Re s\ne\tfrac12$, e.g.\ $s=-2$; but these are the \emph{trivial} zeros inherited from $\zeta$, unrelated to the twist.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{M\"obius Riemann hypothesis} asserts that every zero $s$ of $\zt$ that is neither $1$ nor of the form $-2(n+1)$, $n\in\mathbb{N}$, satisfies $\Re s=\tfrac12$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
The M\"obius Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to the classical Riemann hypothesis.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling is not squaring]
$\zt(2)=\pi^2/3\ne\pi^4/36=\zeta(2)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The general phenomenon}

\begin{definition}
A map $N:A\to\mathbb{N}$ is a \emph{$k$-fold norm} ($k\ge1$) if $|\{x : N(x)=n\}|=k$ for every $n\ne0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Oriented zeta theorem]\label{thm:kfold}
If $N:A\to\mathbb{N}$ is a $k$-fold norm then, for $\Re s>1$,
\[\sum_{\substack{x\in A\\ N(x)\ne0}}\frac{1}{N(x)^{s}}\;=\;k\,\zeta(s).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose bijections $N^{-1}(n)\cong\{1,\dots,k\}$ for $n\ge1$; assembling them gives $\{x : N(x)\ne0\}\cong\{1,\dots,k\}\times\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ under which the summand depends only on the second coordinate. Reindexing an absolutely convergent sum over a finite product multiplies it by the size of the finite factor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No $k$-fold cover moves a zero]
For each $k\ge1$, the assertion that every non-trivial, non-pole zero of $k\zeta$ lies on $\Re s=\tfrac12$ is equivalent to the classical Riemann hypothesis. Every $k\ge1$ is realised by some $k$-fold norm (take $A=\mathbb{N}\times\{1,\dots,k\}$, $N(n,i)=n$), so the constant is sharp.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
The M\"obius norm is a $2$-fold norm, and $\zt=2\zeta$ is the case $k=2$: the doubling is exactly the order of the orientation group, entering as a scalar and never as an Euler-factor multiplicity.
\end{corollary}

\section{Where the twist lives: the additive obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplication is orientation-local]\label{thm:mullift}
For all oriented integers $a=(m,\varepsilon)$, $b=(n,\delta)$: $[a]\cdot[b]=[(mn,\varepsilon\delta)]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Additive obstruction]\label{thm:addobs}
There is no pair of functions $g:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{Z}$, $h:\{\pm1\}^2\to\{\pm1\}$ with $[a]+[b]=[(g(m,n),h(\varepsilon,\delta))]$ for all $a=(m,\varepsilon)$, $b=(n,\delta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $a=(1,+1)$ and successively $b=(1,+1)$, $b=(1,-1)$. The sums are $2$ and $0$; but the class $[(g(1,1),\eta)]$ has absolute signed value $|g(1,1)|$ regardless of $\eta$, so $|g(1,1)|=2$ and $|g(1,1)|=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp form]
Even allowing the orientation of the sum to be an arbitrary function of both complete oriented inputs, the magnitude of a M\"obius sum cannot be computed from the two magnitudes alone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The argument above never used separability of $h$, only that the absolute signed value of $[(k,\eta)]$ is $|k|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
A binary operation $F:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{Z}$ \emph{lifts separably} if there are $g:\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{Z}$, $h:\{\pm1\}^2\to\{\pm1\}$ with $v(g(m,n),h(\varepsilon,\delta))=F(v(m,\varepsilon),v(n,\delta))$ for all oriented inputs.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Necessary criterion]
If $F$ lifts separably then $|F(-m,n)|=|F(m,n)|$ and $|F(m,-n)|=|F(m,n)|$ for all $m,n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides equal $|g(m,n)|$, since $|v(k,\eta)|=|k|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
Multiplication lifts separably. Addition does not ($|{-1}+1|=0\ne2$); nor does subtraction ($|1-(-1)|=2\ne0$); nor $(m,n)\mapsto m+n^2$; nor even $(m,n)\mapsto m+1$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[The criterion is not sufficient]
Let $F(m,n)=mn$ if $2\mid m$ and $F(m,n)=|mn|$ otherwise. Then $|F(-m,n)|=|F(m,n)|$ and $|F(m,-n)|=|F(m,n)|$ for all $m,n$, yet $F$ admits no separable lift.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The invariances follow from $|F(m,n)|=|mn|$. For the failure: at first-argument magnitude $1$ the two orientation inputs both yield the value $1$, forcing the same output sign; at magnitude $2$ the same orientation inputs must yield $2$ and $-2$. No choice of $h$ satisfies both.
\end{proof}

The obstruction is a sign cocycle, not a size condition.

\begin{theorem}[Classification of twists]\label{thm:twists}
Let $\rho:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}$ be additive with $\rho^{\circ k}=\mathrm{id}$ for some $k>0$. Then either $\rho=\mathrm{id}$, or $\rho(n)=-n$ for all $n$ and $k$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An additive endomorphism of $\mathbb{Z}$ is multiplication by $c=\rho(1)$, and $\rho^{\circ k}$ by $c^{k}$; so $c^{k}=1$, $c=\pm1$, and $c=-1$ forces $k$ even.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There is no genuine $\mathbb{Z}/k$-twist of the integer line of odd order; the M\"obius half-twist is the unique nontrivial finite twist.
\end{corollary}

\section{The oriented double: a twist that survives}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{oriented double} is the group ring of the orientation group,
\[\Oo=\mathbb{Z}[\tau]/(\tau^2-1)\;\cong\;\{(u,v)\in\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z} : u\equiv v\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 2)\}\subseteq\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z},\]
via $a+b\tau\mapsto(a+b,a-b)$. The diagonal $\iota:\mathbb{Z}\to\Oo$, $n\mapsto(n,n)$, is an injective ring map, and $\tau=(1,-1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Basis]
Every element of $\Oo$ is uniquely $\iota(a)+\iota(b)\tau$ with $a,b\in\mathbb{Z}$, and $\tau^2=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Not a domain]
$(1+\tau)(1-\tau)=0$ while $1+\tau\ne0\ne1-\tau$. Hence $\Oo$ is not a domain, and $\Oo\not\cong\mathbb{Z}$, $\Oo\not\cong\Zt$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The orientation group grows]
$x\in\Oo$ is a unit iff $x\in\{1,-1,\tau,-\tau\}$, and these are distinct; so $\Oo^{\times}$ is the Klein four-group.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{deck involution} $\sigma:\Oo\to\Oo$ is the coordinate swap $(u,v)\mapsto(v,u)$, i.e.\ $\tau\mapsto-\tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed ring]
$\sigma(x)=x$ iff $x=\iota(n)$ for some $n\in\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exactly two orientations]
Let $\pi^{+}(a+b\tau)=a+b$ and $\pi^{-}(a+b\tau)=a-b$. Then $\pi^{+}\ne\pi^{-}$, every ring homomorphism $\Oo\to\mathbb{Z}$ equals $\pi^{+}$ or $\pi^{-}$, and $\pi^{+}\circ\sigma=\pi^{-}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A ring map $f$ is determined by $f(\tau)$, and $f(\tau)^2=1$ forces $f(\tau)=\pm1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The spectrum is a branched double cover}

Let $\pi^{\pm}_p:\Oo\to\mathbb{F}_p$ be the reduction of $\pi^{\pm}$ modulo $p$, and set $P^{\pm}(p)=\ker\pi^{\pm}_p$.

\begin{theorem}
For every prime $p$, $\pi^{\pm}_p$ are surjective, so $P^{\pm}(p)$ are maximal with residue field $\mathbb{F}_p$, and both contract to $p\mathbb{Z}$ along $\iota$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting at odd primes]
For $p$ odd prime, $P^{+}(p)\ne P^{-}(p)$, $P^{+}(p)\cap P^{-}(p)=(p)$, and $\Oo/p\Oo\cong\mathbb{F}_p\times\mathbb{F}_p$. There are exactly two points of $\Spec\Oo$ over $p$, exchanged by $\sigma$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Ramification at $2$]
$P^{+}(2)=P^{-}(2)=:P(2)$, and $P(2)^2\subseteq(2)\subsetneq P(2)$. The residue ring at $2$ is not reduced: the image of $\tau-1$ is a nonzero nilpotent. There is exactly one point of $\Spec\Oo$ over $2$, fixed by $\sigma$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Sheet count]\label{cor:sheets}
Writing $\sh(p)$ for the number of points of $\Spec\Oo$ above $p$,
\[\sh(p)=\begin{cases}1 & p=2,\\ 2 & p\text{ odd}.\end{cases}\]
Thus $\Spec\Oo\to\Spec\mathbb{Z}$ is a double cover branched exactly at $2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Conductor equals branch locus]
$\Oo$ has index two in its normalisation $\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}$: the parity map $(u,v)\mapsto u-v\bmod2$ induces $(\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z})/\Oo\cong\mathbb{Z}/2$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The spectral zeta function of the oriented double}

\begin{definition}
Give each point of $\Spec\Oo$ over $p$ the Euler factor $(1-p^{-s})^{-1}$ and set
\[\zeta_{\Oo}(s)=\prod_{p}\Bigl((1-p^{-s})^{-1}\Bigr)^{\sh(p)} .\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:spectral}
For $\Re s>1$,
\[\zeta_{\Oo}(s)\;=\;\zeta(s)^{2}\,\bigl(1-2^{-s}\bigr).\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary \ref{cor:sheets} the product is $\prod_p(1-p^{-s})^{-2}$ corrected at $p=2$, where the exponent is $1$ rather than $2$; the correction factor is $1-2^{-s}$, nonvanishing for $\Re s>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\zeta_{\Oo}(s)\ne0$ for $\Re s>1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
For $n\ge1$ set $c(n)=d(n)-d(n/2)$, with $d(n/2)$ read as $0$ for odd $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Dirichlet expansion]
For $\Re s>1$, $\zeta_{\Oo}(s)=\sum_{n\ge1}c(n)n^{-s}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-negativity and prime values]
$c(n)\ge0$ for all $n$; $c(p)=2$ for odd primes $p$; $c(2^{k})=1$ for all $k\ge1$. The first twelve values are
\[c(1),\dots,c(12)=1,\,1,\,2,\,1,\,2,\,2,\,2,\,1,\,3,\,2,\,2,\,2 .\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Non-negativity: $n/2\mid n$ so $d(n/2)\le d(n)$. For odd $p$: $d(p)=2$ and the correction vanishes. For $n=2^{k+1}$: $(k+2)-(k+1)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The coefficients count ideals at prime index]
For every prime $p$, the number of ideals $I\subseteq\Oo$ with $|\Oo/I|=p$ equals $c(p)$: two ($P^{+}(p),P^{-}(p)$) for odd $p$, and one ($P(2)$) for $p=2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
$c(9)=3$, matching the three ideals of index $9$: $P^{+}(3)^2$, $P^{+}(3)P^{-}(3)=(3)$, $P^{-}(3)^2$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Value at $s=2$]
$\zeta_{\Oo}(2)=\pi^{4}/48\approx2.02937$, whereas $\zt(2)=\pi^{2}/3\approx3.28987$; the two twists are analytically distinct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[A new zero]\label{thm:newzero}
Let $s_0=2\pi i/\log2$, so $\Re s_0=0$ and $\Imm s_0\approx9.0647$. Then $2^{-s_0}=e^{-2\pi i}=1$, hence $1-2^{-s_0}=0$ and $\zeta_{\Oo}(s_0)=0$ (via the meromorphic continuation of Theorem \ref{thm:spectral}). But $\zeta(s_0)\ne0$: the only zeros of $\zeta$ with $\Re s\le0$ are the real trivial zeros. So $s_0$ is a zero of $\zeta_{\Oo}$ that is not a zero of $\zeta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The oriented Riemann hypothesis is false]
$s_0$ is a zero of $\zeta_{\Oo}$ with $s_0\ne1$, not a trivial zero of $\zeta$, and $\Re s_0=0\ne\tfrac12$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The critical strip is untouched]
All the extra zeros lie on $\Re s=0$. Consequently, inside the open strip $0<\Re s<1$, the oriented Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to the classical one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
On the strip, $1-2^{-s}$ does not vanish, so the zeros of $\zeta(s)^2(1-2^{-s})$ there are exactly the zeros of $\zeta$ with multiplicity doubled.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The dichotomy}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
 & Set-level twist $\Zt$ & Multiplicative twist $\Oo$\\
\midrule
Underlying cover & free, unramified & branched at $2$\\
Ring & $\cong\mathbb{Z}$; PID, domain & not a domain; $\not\cong\mathbb{Z}$\\
Unit group & $\mathbb{Z}/2$ & Klein four-group\\
Prime elements over $p$ & two ($\mathbb{Z}/2$-torsor) & ---\\
Spectrum points over $p$ & one & two for odd $p$, one for $p=2$\\
Euler factors & unchanged (global factor $2$) & squared, corrected at $2$\\
Zeta function & $2\zeta(s)$ & $\zeta(s)^2(1-2^{-s})$\\
Value at $s=2$ & $\pi^2/3$ & $\pi^4/48$\\
New zeros & none & $2\pi ik/\log2$, $k\ne0$\\
Riemann hypothesis & equivalent to classical & false; equivalent on the strip\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The conceptual statement: a $\mathbb{Z}/2$-symmetry realised as an identification of the underlying set is multiplicatively trivialisable (Corollary \ref{cor:split}) and additively non-liftable (Theorem \ref{thm:addobs}); triviality forces the ring to be untwisted and the zeta function to be a scalar multiple. A $\mathbb{Z}/2$-symmetry realised in the multiplication is not trivialisable and produces a branched cover of the spectrum with the corresponding Euler-factor multiplicities. The boundary between the regimes is exactly the failure of addition to lift.

\subsection{Verdict on the original conjectures}

Class number one: true, but inherited. Primes splitting into $p^{+},p^{-}$: true on elements, false on the spectrum; the genuine splitting occurs in $\Oo$. Two factorizations of $6$: true as oriented data (exactly four ordered ones), not a failure of unique factorization. Unique factorization up to orientation: true, sharply (Corollary \ref{cor:radii}). Non-Ore: false. Zeros off the critical line: for $\Zt$ only the inherited trivial ones, with the Riemann hypothesis unchanged; for $\Oo$ genuinely new zeros supplied by ramification, but confined to $\Re s=0$.

\subsection{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. M\"obius normal form and arithmetic.} Represent $x$ by $V(x)$; reduce $(n,\varepsilon)$ by computing $\varepsilon n$; add and multiply signed values. $O(1)$ bignum operations per step.

\textbf{B. Oriented prime factorization.} Given $x\ne0$: factor $N(x)=\prod p_i^{e_i}$ classically; the oriented factorizations are the orientation assignments whose product matches the orientation of $x$, giving $2^{\Omega(N)-1}$ multisets, all associate in pairs, with a unique multiset of radii.

\textbf{C. Sheet counting for the oriented double.} Sieve $d$ up to $X$ in $O(X\log X)$ and form $c(n)=d(n)-d(n/2)$; partial sums approximate $\zeta_{\Oo}(s)$, and $c(p)=\sh(p)$.

\subsection{Applications and future work}

$\Oo$ is the simplest non-maximal order in the split \'etale algebra $\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}$, and the computations here --- conductor $2$, split residue rings at odd primes, non-reduced residue ring at $2$, zeta function $\zeta^2(1-2^{-s})$ --- are an explicit instance of the zeta theory of orders, with the M\"obius framing reading the conductor as a branch locus. Theorem \ref{thm:kfold} formalises a negative principle of independent use: a covering whose fibre cardinality is constant contributes an additive constant to $\log\zeta$ and can never move a zero. The dichotomy ``multiplication lifts, addition does not'' is a statement about sign-magnitude representations of signed quantities.

Open directions: (i) the analogous analysis for $\mathbb{Z}[t]/(t^k-1)$, branched exactly at the primes dividing $k$; (ii) non-abelian orientation groups, where non-commutative orders make the Ore condition a genuine question; (iii) for a general order in an \'etale $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra, determine which zeros are created by the conductor --- Theorem \ref{thm:newzero} is the $\mathbb{Z}[\mathbb{Z}/2]$ case; (iv) a cohomological classification of the binary operations that lift separably to the oriented cover.

\section{Conclusion}

The M\"obius half-twist is real at the level of the cover --- free, fixed-point-free, exactly two sheets --- and survives multiplicatively as a split $\mathbb{Z}/2$ of orientations, giving prime elements in pairs, a doubled divisor function, and the zeta function $2\zeta(s)$. But it does not survive as \emph{ring} structure: the ring of M\"obius integers is $\mathbb{Z}$, its spectrum is $\Spec\mathbb{Z}$, its class number is one for the least interesting reason, and its Riemann hypothesis is the ordinary one. The obstruction is precisely the failure of addition to lift, and this failure is sharp.

Replacing the identification by a multiplicative twist repairs every deficiency at once. The oriented double is not a domain, has a larger orientation group, and its spectrum is a genuine double cover of $\Spec\mathbb{Z}$ branched exactly at $2$. Its zeta function squares the Euler factors, its Dirichlet coefficients $d(n)-d(n/2)$ count ideals, and its branch locus supplies new zeros at $2\pi ik/\log2$ which nonetheless leave the critical strip undisturbed. Topology suggested the question; the answer is that a topological twist becomes an arithmetic one exactly when it is written into the multiplication.

